  body: "# State Pension remand and the pending criminal outcome\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nThe
    captured State Pension passage distinguishes remand from its prisoner definition, qualifies payment
    by the later criminal outcome and directs suspension pending that outcome.\n\n## Scope and exceptions\n\
    \nDMG 12020 must be read with the full prisoner definition in DMG 12018 and the specified later sentences.
    It does not promise payment throughout remand, treat all detention alike or state current law. This
    is payment suspension in the remand context, not the separate voluntary suspension of State Pension
    entitlement.\n\n## Inspected source passages\n\n- [DMG 12018, PDF page 4](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/651bcf2e6dfda6000d8e39d2/dmgch12.pdf#page=4)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 12: Imprisonment.\n- [DMG 12020, PDF page 5](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/651bcf2e6dfda6000d8e39d2/dmgch12.pdf#page=5)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 12: Imprisonment.\n\n## Evidence and review status\n\nModel-assisted interpretation
    of selected passages in the 15 September 2026 frozen source snapshot. Specialist review is required.
    Source acquisition, source revision, legal effective dates and this record’s authoring time are separate.\n\
    \nThis is an independent experimental publication, not an official DWP document, individual advice,
    an entitlement decision or an executable rule. Machine-extracted text may alter reading order, footnotes
    and characters. No PDF visual inspection, whole-chapter coverage or behavioural evaluation pass is
    claimed by this record.\n\n- `dmg-vol3-ch12`: PDF SHA-256 `be79f9d6f8baf23290b8d6af5f384d31c2435b960fe3a59c14febd510fbaa9fe`;
    extracted pages SHA-256 `989a5b292ba1f6129362ddc7ee736133798156d58c3539be2d0232fad5c92660`.\n"
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/dmg-payment-suspension
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The remand paragraph expressly directs suspension pending the outcome; the general
      payment-suspension concept is a procedural research link, not proof that every suspension uses
      the same power.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch12/0005
      quote: "12020 A person who is remanded in custody is not a prisoner1 in accordance with DMG 12018
        so can be\npaid SP unless\n\n\n1. a sentence of imprisonment or detention in legal custody as
        a result of criminal proceedings 2or\n\n\n2. a sentence of detention where paragraph 12018 3.1
        or 4. applies3or\n\n\n3. a suspended sentence of imprisonment4\n\n\nis later imposed on the person
        for the offence5. Therefore DMs should suspend SP when a person is\nremanded in custody pending
        the outcome of proceedings.\n\n\n                   1 SP Regs, reg 3(1); 2 reg 3(2)(a); 3 reg
        3(2)(b); 4 reg 3(2)(c); Criminal Justice Act 03, s\n                   189;\n                \
        \   5 SP Regs, reg 3(1)"
      locator: DMG 12020
